{
 "_doc": "THE KEYS THE REGISTER DEAL'S NAME POOL READS (T-1502). tools/replace_invented_residents.py refuses a documented man a reconstructed roof when his surname is 'already named in the town' — when this project has already said something about that name. Until T-1502 the pool was every capitalised word in the RAW TEXT of every structure record, every household card outside the deal's own hh_inf_ layer, and data/exclusions.json. That reads prose on purpose (most of what the project says about a name lives in a note), but it also read whatever ANY pass wrote into those files, so a pass putting a proper name into a card for another reason — T-1489 wrote a house's business_name beside its id and retired the tailor Thomas S. Eels — silently took a man out of the deal. Now the pool reads a capitalised word only under a key path listed here. `read` is the list, per record kind; a path is written with its dict keys joined by '.' and a list step as '[]'. `ignored` maps a path to the reason its words are NOT names the town has said anything about, and keeps them out of the pool. `replace_invented_residents.py --check` fails when a string under a path that is in neither list carries a capitalised word, and names the kind, the path, a file and the word: declare the path read (it is something the project SAYS about a name) or ignored (it is a label, a pointer, a copy) — the choice is the point, and it is made at the writer's PR instead of by a stranger's DRIFT line. SEEDED 2026-09-24 from the committed tree: all 275 paths that carried a capitalised word, every one `read`, so the pool is the raw-text pool exactly (3,127 words) and no seat or refusal changed. Whether every one of them SHOULD be read — persons[].workplaces[].business_name has the shape of the path that retired Eels — is reported by --report and not re-ruled here.",
